    "turn_idx": 152,
    "turn_text": "So I'm not as optimistic about Gen Z. When you look at by the numbers, they're less likely to go to church. They're less likely to settle down and actually get married and have a family. They're less likely to start a business. Obviously, it's still young. Gen Z, I'm just looking at it, tops out today would be 26 years old, the oldest Gen Z-er. I'm not as optimistic because the early data that we have does not suggest that they will be taking positions and taking stances in life that would lend themselves to developing a conservative mindset, right? So if they're less likely to start a business, they're less likely to see what the tax structure looks like, and they're less likely to understand how burdensome that is. If they're less likely to go to church, they're less likely to become pro-life. They're less likely to have to hold religious positions on different issues, right? less likely to get married, less likely to have kids, less likely to have a family and understand what it means to preserve the country for the next generation coming up. So I'm not as optimistic about your generation. The one thing I am optimistic about is that Every generation has a counterculture. You had the hippies going against the grain. With your generation, one of the countercultures is actually being on the right because society itself has swung so much further to left. So there is a counterculture in your generation that is against unlimited genders, a whole spectrum. that's against the police who are policing thought and policing language. That is what I am enthusiastic about. Now, if there's enough of them, I don't know if there's enough of them to swing the tide, but there were enough hippies back in the day to affect politics. Hopefully, there's enough counterculture Republicans in your generation or conservatives in your generation to move the needle.",
